  "notes": "Hard-edged \"redneck woman\" country-rock — the loudest female radio voice of the mid-2000s. The arrangement claim is a drum parameter here and is authored as one: \"the party cuts leave the kit at full intensity from bar 1,\" so the main velocity tier sits higher than any other model in this batch and there is no quiet verse tier, with cross-stick reserved for the ballads only. The rest is the stated grammar — \"a hard rock backbeat and quarter kick\" and \"gang-shout chorus layers are structural,\" the latter authored as a snap/clap perc lane. Harmony is \"I–IV–V and I–♭VII–IV; the ballads use vi–IV–I–V.\" The bass is quoted: \"live overdriven bass in 8ths, doubling guitar riffs,\" so `independent_riff` with root-following dropped. Melody is \"belted mid-alto with blues bends, narrow range, chant hooks; the chorus is designed as a shouted crowd response,\" which is the high cell repeat and the blues scale weight against a narrow register. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF] and the entry notes the signature cut's 188 reading folds to ~94. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
